00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.690
As you may have noticed,
most of the topics

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.600
we cover on this series
come from real-world issues

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.610
that customers and
developers are running into.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.040
For example, Ed
Robinson here, asking

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.110
for some content on how to
profile Google Cloud Functions.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:25.430
Ed, thanks for watching.

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:27.260
Here's what we've got for you.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.380
Google Cloud Functions
is a serverless offering

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.540
on Google Cloud
that's still in beta,

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.070
at the time of this recording.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.750
As such, there are still some
things that need ironing out.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.020
But in the meantime,
here's some great ways

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.950
to get the info on the
performance of your functions.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.700
In order to get a base
level understanding

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.590
of how your GCF
code is performing,

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.210
there's two things to look at.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.310
First, you can view
your Cloud Functions

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:50.860
with their execution
times and execution counts

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:53.327
in the Cloud Platform Console.

00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:55.660
From here, you can get aggregate
performance information

00:00:55.660 --> 00:00:58.000
about how long it took
to deploy your function,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.770
as well as how long it took
to respond to a request.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.260
Now, there may be
situations where

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.840
you need to output some
content to your logs

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.200
in order to help debug
or mark specific events.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.260
Calling the console.log
function in GCF

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.570
outputs a line to
the logs, which you

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:15.370
can view in the GCP Console.

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:17.890
At the time of this video,
full Stackdriver Tracing

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.671
is not integrated
with functions.

00:01:19.671 --> 00:01:21.670
However, if you're looking
for more information,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:23.920
there's a couple of things
you can do until then.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.110
First is inline
function wrapping.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.005
Since GCF logs can be
read via the gsutil API,

00:01:30.005 --> 00:01:32.380
it's straightforward to wrap
core parts of your functions

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.210
with timer statements
and log those durations.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:36.880
Later, you can walk
through that data

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:38.890
and check out how
each area is behaving

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.740
or import all of your
logging data into BigQuery

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.060
and do even deeper analysis.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.550
Second is emulator timings.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:47.860
Now, it turns out that
you can do quite a bit

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.260
to profile your code
locally through the emulator

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:51.570
before actually deploying it.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.960
Now, technology
like the V8 Profiler

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.050
can help a ton
here, although you

00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:58.510
might need to modify your
code to get it all working.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.250
And of course, the
execution times

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:02.190
won't be exact since
your local machine may

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.940
have different performance
characteristics

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:04.940
than those in the cloud.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:06.520
But you can still
get a good sense

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:07.760
of where the hotspots are.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.759
If you'd like more
information about these tools

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:11.925
and some other ways to
profile your Cloud Functions,

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:14.540
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.660
And in the coming weeks,
I'll be taking a look

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.960
at a few of the most common
GCF performance issues here

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:21.580
on Cloud Performance Atlas.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.700
So stay tuned, because
every millisecond counts.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:27.150
